Redefining the trigger to require only "no GDP decline in the past 8 quarters" is not enough. I backtested it (fixtest.py) and slow recoveries still meet it: US 1977-09, 1985 and 2013-09; CAN 1977; ESP 1997 and 2018; AUS 1994.

A definition that matches the paper's own eleven-episode evidence adds two conditions to the 2025 level. The trigger is met when all three hold:
- the 12-month average is at or above the 2025 average plus 3;
- the 12-month average is at least 3 points above its own value 24 months earlier;
- no completed quarter in those 24 months had real GDP below its previous peak.

Without the 2025-level condition, this backtest (fixtest2.py) gives zero historical hits in all nine countries with data. That would make "the United States has not seen" true and the "Checkable soon" trigger rows a real test of the AI-shock assumption.

If the definition is kept instead:
- drop "separates ... from an ordinary recession", "without a recession", "keeps growing", "has not seen" and "leaves output growing";
- re-anchor shock_by_* as the union of AI-shock and recession-recovery paths (historical US base rate of about 8%, 19% and 52% at 3, 5 and 15 years), and reconsider the shock-state endorsement and amount inputs for recession-triggered histories;
- relabel the column "By 2040 if the US trigger is not met".

In either case, say that Brazil's history is unchecked, or fetch PNAD from 2012 and re-run the backtest.

